UN1CA sm7125 kernel: KernelSU-Next + security fixes
===================================================

This is the UN1CA Android ROM kernel for the Samsung sm7125 platform
(a52q / a72q = Galaxy A52 / A72), Linux 4.14.357-openela, branch "sixteen".
On top of the stock tree it adds, entirely through scripts so the result is
reproducible by re-running them:

  * KernelSU-Next (root), pinned to the v3.1.0-legacy line that supports this
    non-GKI 4.14 kernel, applied as manual hooks.
  * Backports of two security fixes the tree does not have yet: Dirty Frag
    (CVE-2026-43284) and Fragnesia (CVE-2026-46300).
  * A flashable boot.img and a repacked, signed UN1CA ROM ZIP.
  * A separate, userspace-only KernelSU module that fixes USB file transfer
    (MTP) being dead after a fresh boot on UN1CA.

Nothing edits the kernel or KernelSU-Next source in place: every change is an
embedded patch an "apply" script lays on the working tree and can revert. The
only thing any script downloads is KernelSU-Next itself (and, optionally, a
prebuilt ramdisk). The build environment is NOT installed by these scripts;
they only check for it and tell you the apt command to run if something is
missing.


Layout
------

  apply_ksu.sh             Add / check / remove KernelSU-Next (manual hooks).
  apply_security_fixes.sh  Add / check / remove the CVE backports.
  build_kernel.sh          Build the tree as it currently is -> dist/<device>/.
  build_all.sh             apply_ksu + apply_security_fixes + build, then
                           revert whatever it applied. One command, clean tree.
  patch_rom_zip.sh         Build the kernel and repack it into a signed UN1CA
                           flashable ROM ZIP.
  build_usb_fix_module.sh  Generate the USB-boot-fix KernelSU module ZIP.
  tests/skb_coalesce/      Normal-path functional test (kernel module) for the
                           Fragnesia skb_try_coalesce() change.
  test-keys/               AOSP public test key used for signing. Stored here
                           on purpose; the scripts never download keys.
  dist/                    Build outputs (git-ignored except usb-boot-fix/).

Run every script from the top of the kernel tree. Each takes -h / --help.


Build environment (install once, machine-wide)
----------------------------------------------

The scripts assume a Debian/Ubuntu host and install nothing. If anything is
missing they stop and print the exact apt line. The dependencies are, from the
official Debian repositories:

  sudo apt install git make python3 bc bison flex perl clang lld llvm \
                   mkbootimg openssl libssl-dev zip signapk

  * git make python3 bc bison flex perl clang lld llvm libssl-dev  - kernel build
  * mkbootimg openssl                                              - boot.img pack + AVB signing
  * signapk openssl                                                - ROM ZIP signing (patch_rom_zip.sh)
  * zip                                                            - USB fix module (build_usb_fix_module.sh)

The test key lives in test-keys/ (download it once yourself if it is absent;
see test-keys/README). It is the public AOSP test key, used to re-sign the
things UN1CA signs with its own non-public key, so a self-built image verifies.


Building
--------

Everything, in one go (KernelSU-Next + security fixes), then a clean tree:

  ./build_all.sh --device both --ramdisk download

  --device    a52q | a72q | both      (asked if omitted)
  --ramdisk   extract | download | skip
                extract   pull the ramdisk, version string, patch level and
                          layout from a stock boot.img you provide with
                          --boot-img <dev>=<path>
                download  fetch a prebuilt ramdisk
                skip      no ramdisk (kernel-only Image)
  --ksu-ref <ref>   build a specific KernelSU-Next commit / tag / branch
  --clean           wipe out/ first

Output lands in dist/<device>/ : boot.img (signed, flashable), Image,
dtbo.img, kernel.release. The kernel version string (uname -r) always ends in
"-g<commit>" so a build is identifiable and never collides with stock.

Finer control - do the steps yourself:

  ./apply_ksu.sh                      # add KernelSU-Next to the working tree
  ./apply_security_fixes.sh           # add the CVE backports
  ./build_kernel.sh --device a52q --ramdisk download
  ./apply_security_fixes.sh --revert  # undo (any order)
  ./apply_ksu.sh --revert

  ./apply_ksu.sh --status             # exit 0 = applied, 3 = not
  ./apply_security_fixes.sh --status  # per-fix state; a fix the tree already
                                      # carries is detected and skipped

build_kernel.sh builds the tree exactly as it is, so apply only what you want
in a given image. build_all.sh is the convenience wrapper that applies both,
builds, and reverts on exit (leaving anything you had applied beforehand).


Repacking a flashable ROM ZIP
-----------------------------

To drop the patched kernel into a UN1CA flashable ZIP (only boot.img is
replaced; every other entry is copied and CRC-checked, then the ZIP is signed
with the test key and verified):

  ./patch_rom_zip.sh UN1CA_3.2.1-..._a52q-sign.zip

It reads the device from the ZIP, extracts its boot.img, has build_all.sh
build against it, and writes dist/<name>-ksu.zip. Use --as-is to package the
tree as it currently is (whatever you applied yourself) instead of the full
KSU + fixes set; its output name ends in -custom. See --help for -o / --clean.


USB file-transfer (MTP) boot fix
--------------------------------

On UN1CA, USB is charge-only after a fresh boot with every mode option greyed
out; toggling USB debugging "kicks" it and it works until the next reboot. The
gadget simply never binds its UDC at boot (the boot config includes adb but
FunctionFS isn't ready, and UsbDeviceManager never retries). This is a ROM /
userspace init race, not a kernel gap, so the fix is userspace-only and does
not depend on the kernel build.

  ./build_usb_fix_module.sh               # -> dist/usb-boot-fix/*.zip
  ./build_usb_fix_module.sh --config mtp  # auto File-transfer every boot
                                          # (convenient, small security cost)

Install the ZIP from the KernelSU-Next manager (Modules -> Install from
storage) and reboot. Its boot service waits for boot to finish and, only if
the gadget is still unbound, replays the same restart the debugging toggle
does, so USB data works and "File transfer" is selectable normally. Per-boot
log: /data/adb/modules/un1ca_usb_boot_fix/usb_boot_fix.log .


Testing the Fragnesia fix
-------------------------

tests/skb_coalesce builds a kernel module that exercises skb_try_coalesce() on
normal input and checks that the shared-frag marker is carried over (what the
fix adds) without being invented. Build and sign it against the same out/ that
produced the flashed kernel, then load it on that device:

  ./tests/skb_coalesce/run.sh
  adb push tests/skb_coalesce/skb_coalesce_test.ko /data/local/tmp/
  adb shell su -c 'insmod /data/local/tmp/skb_coalesce_test.ko; \
                   dmesg | grep skb_coalesce_test; rmmod skb_coalesce_test'


================================================================================
Original kernel README below
================================================================================

Linux kernel
============

This file was moved to Documentation/admin-guide/README.rst

Please notice that there are several guides for kernel developers and users.
These guides can be rendered in a number of formats, like HTML and PDF.

In order to build the documentation, use ``make htmldocs`` or
``make pdfdocs``.

There are various text files in the Documentation/ subdirectory,
several of them using the Restructured Text markup notation.
See Documentation/00-INDEX for a list of what is contained in each file.

Please read the Documentation/process/changes.rst file, as it contains the
requirements for building and running the kernel, and information about
the problems which may result by upgrading your kernel.
